# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Primer — .gitignore  (Django + DRF + Celery + React/Vite)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ===== Secrets & environment (NEVER commit) =====
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
secrets/
backend/config/settings/local.py

# Google service-account keys (Vertex AI — Docs/Hosting.md §3). A safety net, not
# the plan: the key belongs **outside the repository entirely**
# (`~/.config/primer/vertex-sa.json`), because a rule here only protects the
# filenames somebody thought of. One was found sitting at the repo root as
# `gemini-key.json`, untracked but visible to `git status` and one `git add -A`
# away from a history that cannot be rewritten — `*.pem` and `*.key` above did
# not match it, and a service-account key is a JSON file.
#
# The list below was extended after exactly the failure it predicts. A browser
# download had left `vertex-sa_(1).json` beside `vertex-sa.json` on the deploy
# box, and `*-sa.json` does not match it — the name ends `_(1).json`. The same
# check found `env-for-server.txt`, a production env file that `.env*` misses
# because somebody sensibly gave it a describing name instead of a dotfile one.
# Both were one `git add -A` from a history that cannot be rewritten.
#
# So these patterns are **prefix-anchored and suffix-free**: a rule that ends in
# `.json` only protects the copy nobody renamed, and every real leak starts with
# a rename — a `(1)`, a `.bak`, a `.old`, a timestamp.
*service-account*
*service_account*
vertex-sa*
gemini-key*
gcp-credentials*
*-sa.json
env-for-server*

# ===== Python / Django =====
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
# NOTE: these Python build artifacts are anchored to backend/ on purpose.
# A bare `build/` / `dist/` would also match docs/Build/ on case-insensitive
# filesystems (Windows/macOS) and silently untrack your build briefs.
/backend/build/
develop-eggs/
/backend/dist/
downloads/
eggs/
.eggs/
# NOTE: anchored to backend/ on purpose. A bare `lib/` would also match
# frontend/src/lib/ (the generated API client) and silently untrack it.
/backend/lib/
/backend/lib64/
/backend/parts/
sdist/
/backend/var/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg

# Virtual environments
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
.python-version

# Django runtime
*.log
*.pot
local_settings.py
db.sqlite3
db.sqlite3-journal
# NOTE: user-uploaded media is anchored too — a bare `media/` would match the
# backend/apps/media/ Django app and untrack real source code.
/backend/media/
/backend/staticfiles/
staticfiles/
static_root/
# The warehouse export (`manage.py drain_events`, §5.6). Anchored like the two
# above. These files are real families' telemetry — de-identified, but a running
# log of when actual children played — and the default output directory sits
# inside the repo so the command works on a fresh checkout. Committing one would
# be the quietest possible privacy incident.
/backend/exports/

# Database dumps (`infra/backup/backup.sh`, §16.10). The default destination is
# inside the repo so the restore drill works on a fresh checkout with no setup —
# which makes this line the only thing between a routine drill and a complete
# copy of every family's data, including their encrypted memories, in a git
# history that cannot be rewritten. Real backups belong off this machine
# entirely; see Docs/Runbooks/Backup-Restore.md.
/backups/

# The eval suite's human spot-check output (`pytest evals/ --spotcheck`, §19.7).
# Generated stories, written for a person to read once. Nothing to review in a
# diff, and it accumulates a file per run.
/evals/spotcheck/

# Django migrations: KEEP these in git (they are source of truth for the schema).
# Do NOT ignore migrations/*.py — they must be committed.

# ===== Celery =====
celerybeat-schedule
celerybeat.pid
*.pid

# ===== Testing / coverage / type-check caches =====
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml
.cache/
.mypy_cache/
.dmypy.json
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
.hypothesis/

# ===== Node / React / Vite (frontend build tooling only) =====
node_modules/
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
frontend/dist-ssr/
# The three directories `infra/deploy.sh` shuffles when it swaps the PWA: the
# incoming build, the one it replaced (kept so a rollback is a rename), and the
# one a failed deploy set aside. They only ever exist on the box.
frontend/dist.incoming/
frontend/dist.previous/
frontend/dist.broken/
frontend/.vite/
frontend/coverage/
frontend/stats.html
*.local
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
.eslintcache

# ===== Docker =====
# (compose files & Dockerfiles ARE committed; only ignore local overrides/volumes)
docker-compose.override.yml
.docker/
pgdata/
*.pgdata

# ===== Editors / OS =====
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ===== Misc / local artifacts =====
tmp/
.tmp/
*.bak
export.zip

# ===== Children's voices =====
# Consented reading recordings (Docs/Build/Reading-Integration.md §8). The
# mount point for the container, and never anything git should see: this is the
# most sensitive data the product holds, and the one thing worse than keeping a
# child's voice is committing it. `READING_CORPUS_DIR` points here.
/corpus/

# ===== Operational output pasted from a deployment =====
# Terminal output saved to a scratch file while debugging the box. It is not
# source, and it is one `git add -A` away from being permanent — which matters
# because this output routinely contains live credentials: `docker compose
# config` expands every ${VAR}, and a vendor probe can log a key in a URL. Both
# happened on 15 August 2026 (Docs/Runbooks/Key-Rotation.md).
/prod/
/build-output
/*-output
/*.log.txt

# ===== Safety net: never ignore documentation =====
# docs/ (PRODUCT.md + Build/ + Review/) must always be tracked, even if a
# generic rule above happens to match a folder name like Build/ or dist/.
!docs/
!docs/**


# Deploy bookkeeping, written on the box by infra/deploy.sh: the concurrency
# lock, the last-deploy record, and patches of anything that had been hand-edited
# there before a deploy reset it.
/.deploy/
